# tests/upstream/concurrency.list -- upstream Swift's own tests for the concurrency layer, as the 10.9 gate runs them.
# Every candidate scripts/upstream-tests.py's survey finds in the pinned tree (a test with a RUN: line in
# test/Concurrency/Runtime, or one that REQUIRES concurrency) appears once: `include <path>`, built by
# make-selftest.sh with its compile-line, once per variant (most run twice: the second in Swift 5 mode with
# -strict-concurrency=complete and NonisolatedNonsendingByDefault), and run by run-selftest.sh --gate, or
# `exclude <path> -- <reason>`, a reason a reviewer can check against the test (its RUN: line or a
# REQUIRES:/UNSUPPORTED: is one compile-line refuses, quoted as it says; it uses the Dispatch Swift overlay
# (DispatchQueue and the like), which this release does not ship; it does not compile for 10.9, with the
# error; or it failed on 10.9, with the failure). Tests that are also Darwin or ObjectiveC candidates are
# decided here; those lists point here. build.sh's step 5b checks this list against the tree, so a Swift
# bump that adds a test, or gives an included one a RUN: form, compiler flag or REQUIRES:/UNSUPPORTED: the
# script does not know, stops the build until the list is reviewed (a changed source does not; the gate runs it).
exclude test/Concurrency/Runtime/RuntimeDemangle.swift -- REQUIRES: runtime_module does not hold on 10.9
exclude test/Concurrency/Runtime/actor_assert_precondition_executor.swift -- RUN runs env
exclude test/Concurrency/Runtime/actor_assert_precondition_executor_checkIsolated_bincompat_crash_swift_6_mode.swift -- RUN runs env
